> I think the problem with Nix is that it is actually not a package manager but a build tool (that provides some package management utilities). People try Nix with the assumption that it should be as easy as apt-get (for example), but found tutorials with a block of code in a weird language... Indeed, I've been using Nix for many years (on Ubuntu, NixOS and macOS), and I now find the apt-get style 'considered harmful'. Instead, I define a single Nix derivation ("package") which provides everything I want in its 'bin/' directory, e.g.       with nixpkgs;
    buildEnv 
{
      name = "my-macbook-programs";
      paths = [ bash gimp /* etc */ ];
    }

The nix-build command creates a symlink called `result`, so I put `/path/to/my/git/repo/result/bin` in my $PATH.
